    <briefdescription>
<para>Type-erased interface for appointment-entity CRUD+S operations. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Declares a synchronous callback form and a coroutine (<computeroutput>drogon::Task&lt;void&gt;</computeroutput>) form of every operation. The appointments route registry reaches concrete controllers through this base via a <computeroutput>std::variant</computeroutput>, so its virtual functions are the dispatch point used by a single route handler for all appointment entity types. <verbatim>     In plain words: &quot;synchronous&quot; means the answer arrives through a
</verbatim> callback function, while the coroutine (<computeroutput>drogon::Task&lt;void&gt;</computeroutput>) versions can be paused with co_await so the server can do other work meanwhile. &quot;Virtual
functions&quot; mean a call made through this base runs the real object&apos;s version. A <computeroutput>std::variant</computeroutput> is a box that can hold one of several controller types. <verbatim>     Invocation contract shared by every method:
     - `callback` is `std::function&lt;void(int, const std::string&amp;)&gt;`, it
</verbatim> is moved through the call chain and, by contract, invoked once on each completion path, including failures. Its first argument is an <ref refid="namespaceapi_1_1v2_1_1Http_1a6199aeb84d745378b1befef28d78892f" kindref="member">api::v2::Http::Status</ref> code, its second a response body.<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><computeroutput>requester</computeroutput> is the authenticated caller identity consulted for authorisation.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>data</computeroutput> is a non-owning view of the raw JSON request body, valid only for the call.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>_id</computeroutput> is the optional target id; absence is handled by the concrete implementation. <verbatim>     The `*Async` methods must be co_awaited; the HTTP layer wraps them
</verbatim> in <computeroutput>drogon::async_run</computeroutput> and turns the callback result into a reply. <simplesect kind="note"><para>The interface itself is stateless; behaviour lives in <ref refid="classAppointmentController" kindref="compound">AppointmentController</ref>. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="classAppointmentController" kindref="compound">AppointmentController</ref>, <ref refid="classEntityControllerBase" kindref="compound">EntityControllerBase</ref>, <ref refid="classController" kindref="compound">Controller</ref> </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
